course: dictionary
lesson: 13222
title: Gallantry: bravery and good manners
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Gallantry» is a noun. It means great bravery, especially in battle or danger. Look at the words around it for clues: a rescuer who runs into a fire to save someone is showing gallantry, because the danger is real and the act is brave.
explain: The word also has an older meaning: polite, kind behaviour, such as opening a door or offering a seat. You will mostly meet this in old-fashioned stories. The sentence tells you which meaning is in use: a medal or a dangerous rescue means bravery, while a bow and an opened door mean good manners.
explain: Use the swap check. Put a simple word such as bravery or good manners in place of gallantry and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Gallantry means great bravery, especially in battle or danger.
fact: Gallantry can also mean polite, kind behaviour, such as opening doors for ladies (old-fashioned).
fact: «A medal for gallantry» is given to honour someone's courage.
fact: «The soldier received a medal for gallantry after saving his friends.»
fact: Gallantry is an uncountable noun, so you say «great gallantry», not «a gallantry».
exercise: «The firefighter showed great gallantry when she ran back into the burning house.» What does gallantry mean here? | Tiredness after a long day | Polite behaviour at a dinner party | *Great bravery | Loud shouting
exercise: In an old story, «The knight bowed low and held the door open for the lady, showing gallantry.» What does gallantry mean in this sentence? | Being very tired | *Polite, kind behaviour towards a lady | Anger at the lady | Great bravery in battle
exercise: Why might a medal be given «for gallantry»? | For being the fastest runner in class | To say thank you for a birthday present | For the best cake at the school fair | *To honour courage shown in danger
test: In an old-fashioned story, a man who opens a door and offers a seat is showing what? | Being lost | Great bravery in battle | *Polite, kind behaviour | Loud anger
test: Which word is a good swap for gallantry in «a medal for gallantry»? | Tiredness | Anger | Silence | *Bravery
exam: Which sentence uses gallantry to mean great bravery? | Mum gave gallantry to the dog at teatime. | *The rescuers were honoured for their gallantry in the flood. | We ate gallantry for lunch. | The children showed gallantry by sharing their lunch with a new friend.
